the mother says TSA agents at  dallas-fort Worth International Airport  went too far entreated her family she  says like dogs  Jennifer Williamson posted video to  Facebook showing an agent patting down  her son she says that they were held at  the checkpoint for about an hour causing  them to miss their flight Omar  Villafranca is at the DFW Airport with  an interview that you'll see only on CBS  this morning and the TSA s response  Omar good morning good morning the TSA  says the procedure performed by that  agent on video was part of the new  standard pat-down that went into effect  earlier this month instead of several  variations there's now just one full  body pat-down well that touched a nerve  with the mother who went through  security here at DFW on Sunday with her  son we were treated with utter  disrespect as if we were criminals  Jennifer Williamson turned her anger  into action Sunday recording a TSA  officer patting down her 13-year old son  at dallas-fort Worth International  Airport and posting it on Facebook I  believe he was patted down excessively  they went over his sensitive areas a  little more than necessary especially  given that he wasn't wearing bulky  clothing or anything like that  Williamson says the whole thing started  when agents found a laptop in his book  bag as it went through the scanning  machine they then said her son would  have to submit to a pat-down even though  he did not set off the body scanner she  requested they screen him in other ways  because her son suffers from sensory  processing disorder which makes him  sensitive to touch in the facebook video  the agent explains the procedure and  then packs down his back side front and  down his legs his supervisor who was  observing them then instructs the man to  complete the final step as per policy  the TSA officer uses the back of his  hand for pat downs over sensitive areas  of the body the TSA says the boy  cooperated during the screening process  and all approved procedures were  followed as for the wait time in a  statement the agency says the passengers  were at the checkpoint for approximately  45 minutes which included the time it  took to discuss screening procedures  with the mother and to screen three  carry-on items that required further  inspection his  first question to me was I don't  understand why they did this I don't  know what I did wrong and to me that was  a sign of trauma for him to think that  he had done anything wrong the new  procedures were put in place after a  2015 audit found that there were serious  security lapses Williamson has filed a  complaint with the TSA but has not heard  back charlie Omar thanks
 

